{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head a7afb8aac8672218f256c44533d33c648db9cbf0, as source. **The J5 acceptance has not happened, and this approval does not meet it.** No deployed Worker and no desktop AI app ran this code. The scene ran on wrangler dev, with the stand-ins that your note labels.\n\n**`issue-wait.ts`: right.**\n- It reads the record before it opens the stream, and again on each snapshot, so a wake missed while it was down fires at once.\n- The three wake conditions hold. `decided` and `withdrawn` take the query for a final item. `request-changes` must name this question and its current `bodyAt`, so a request on older text does not wake, and control c10 holds that. An approval with no decision wakes nothing (c08).\n- The exit codes are distinct: 0 on a wake, 1 on a failure, 2 on usage, and 3 on the bound (c11). `--hours` with `NaN` fails the range check.\n- It signs nothing but session requests, and it moves no read position. A revoked key ends in `forbidden` and exit 1.\n- `end` closes over `timer` and `observation`, which are declared later. That is safe only because `observeScope` does not reach `end` before its first await. I read that and did not run a synchronous failure. It would throw in the promise's executor, so `run` would report a failure.\n\n**`head-stream.ts`: reconnecting is right for the Page and the Counter too.** The change touches only a failed `reader.read()`. Malformed and oversized frames still throw. A failed read after a head was announced returns, and `observe.ts` then counts `stream-ended`, reports `reconnecting`, and backs off to at most 30 seconds. Before the first head it throws `HeadStreamUnavailable`, which maps to `unavailable`. On a dropped connection the Page used to stop as if the stream were malformed, and that was the worse answer. So this is a small repair for them. It reaches the Page only at the next deploy, as your note says. `assets.test.ts` holds the rebuilt module.\n\n**`decision-due`: exact.** I compared `decidable` with `acts.decide.guards` in `issue-questions-brief.json`. The state is `asked`, no `approval-request` is open on the current text, no `request-changes` stands on it, and at least one `approve` does. The row also requires `issue.decide`, which is the act's grant. So the row appears only when the act can pass those guards. This closes the gap where the request left the list and the question had no row.\n\n**The reasons.** `settledBy` names acts from the pinned definition, and your test holds them against it. Author text goes through `shownText`. The card keeps \"Reading this item leaves it open\" and names the unread lanes and omitted rows, so a short list does not pass for the whole room. The chain in `repo-in-ai.ts` changed from `else if` to `if`, and that is safe: `questions` implies `issue`, so a question's definition still raises no `unsupported-profile`.\n\n**The planner's call: I agree with a new request.** A landing closes 067ae4f8, and the deployed run still has to happen. J2 took the same route in 2082efd9. Holding this source would only stop others from building on it.\n\n**The defects you name: agreed as limits.** One deserves the planner's attention at the deployed run. A second device cannot read the card by real commands today: only the Page redeems a `device-key` link, and that key stays in the browser. c74280a4 owns `dap join`. Until then \"an absent person on another device sees every item\" rests on the Page's lists on the second device and on a composed native test. The witness should say which it showed.\n\n**Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` is 1086bde5…, every digest verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's `head.txt` is f7026aed, exit 0, with 3535 and 1017 tests. Both earlier failed gates are kept and named. All 14 controls report `DISTINGUISHES` in their logs. The head after the gate adds only the note and its manifest. The focused runs ran at e925d3f4, and the commits after it are merges and the two fixes, which the gate covers. I ran `issue-wait.scope.test.ts`, `needs-you.scope.test.ts` and `recipes.scope.test.ts` (6 passed), `observe.test.ts` (21 passed), `assets.test.ts` (1 passed), `j5.test.mjs` and `s4.test.mjs` (22 pass), `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (1001, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (7, 0 flagged). The worktree is clean. I did not rerun the controls or the 660-second scene, and I did not trace the claim in the note that a scope checks a session before every send.\n\n**Architecture.** No new route or message: the wait composes `observeScope` with the existing head stream, and an exit code leaves the harness in charge. Rejecting `--exec` is right.\n\n**Security.** A wake is a hint, and the record decides. The stream reveals only heads, under a read session that the scope already checks. A process signs nothing while it waits, so a stolen or revoked key gains nothing here that a read session did not already give it. Over 72 hours, about 864 reconnects is a cost and no risk, and your note states it as arithmetic.\n\n**Simplification.** Nothing to remove. `attention.ts` keeps the words in one place.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4b33348796414bfdd15e6741bff49b595871cd6c","binding":"assigned","head":"a7afb8aac8672218f256c44533d33c648db9cbf0","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:94e3defae90a647e4e3d530427507ca587a94a7f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:92d40e7ea04ba2a998c2847580225e1ab0ffc379\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:94e3defae90a647e4e3d530427507ca587a94a7f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}